Classify HTTP 402 (Payment Required) as a provider QUOTA failure instead of
letting it fall through the `status >= 400` catch-all to INVALID_REQUEST.

Target: free-coding-models src/core/router-v2/failure-classifier.js
Package version this was generated against: 0.5.96
Repo: https://github.com/vava-nessa/free-coding-models

Why this matters
----------------
STATUS_KIND_MAP had no 402 entry, so classifyStatus() hit the
`if (status >= 400) return FAILURE_KINDS.INVALID_REQUEST` catch-all. The
INVALID_REQUEST verdict sets:

    blame: 'client', healthDamage: false, failover: true

Consequences when an upstream provider returns 402 (prepaid balance
exhausted / daily cap / per-key spend cap):

  1. fcm told the client "400 invalid_request", i.e. it blamed the
     caller's request payload. Observed: a valid 83k-token request was
     reported as malformed purely because the account balance (9349) was
     smaller than the request.
  2. healthDamage:false meant the circuit breaker never counted the
     failure. Confirmed: exhausted models had no breaker record at all
     while healthy models had 24.
  3. autoHeal could not eject the exhausted models, so fcm kept routing
     to them and burning every model in the set on doomed requests.

Mapping 402 to FAILURE_KINDS.QUOTA yields:

    blame: 'model', healthDamage: true, failover: true,
    quotaPauseMs: <capped Retry-After>, clientStatus: 429

so the breaker trips, autoHeal can replace the model, and the client is
told the truth (a rate/quota condition, not a bad request).

Apply with: patches/apply-fcm-402-patch.sh
Re-apply after every `npm install -g free-coding-models` (it silently
reverts in-place edits to a global package).

--- a/src/core/router-v2/failure-classifier.js
+++ b/src/core/router-v2/failure-classifier.js
@@ -55,6 +55,14 @@
 // busy), not a provider-wide outage.
 const STATUS_KIND_MAP = new Map([
   [401, FAILURE_KINDS.AUTH],
+  // 📖 402 Payment Required is a PROVIDER-side billing condition: prepaid
+  // balance exhausted, daily cap hit, or a per-key spend cap. Without this
+  // entry it fell through to the `status >= 400` catch-all in classifyStatus
+  // and was classified INVALID_REQUEST, which sets blame:'client' and
+  // healthDamage:false. That made fcm report "400 invalid_request" for a
+  // perfectly valid request, never counted the failure toward the circuit
+  // breaker, and never let autoHeal eject the exhausted models.
+  [402, FAILURE_KINDS.QUOTA],
   [403, FAILURE_KINDS.AUTH],
   [408, FAILURE_KINDS.TIMEOUT],
   [429, FAILURE_KINDS.RATE_LIMIT],
